course: dictionary
lesson: 8603
title: Accents and dialects: Scouse
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An accent is the way a person sounds when they speak. A dialect is a local way of speaking, with its own words. Scouse is the accent and dialect of Liverpool, a city in north-west England.
explain: Every accent is a proper way of speaking. Standard English is a tool for formal writing, like letters and school reports, but that does not make other accents wrong. Jory's words show this: «Boss, your nan makes the best chips» is good, clear Scouse.
explain: Use the context to work out a Scouse word. In «Boss, your nan makes the best chips», the chips are being praised, so boss must mean great. Then check: does your meaning still make sense in the sentence?
fact: Scouse is the accent and dialect of Liverpool, in north-west England.
fact: Scouse is also a meat and vegetable stew, a traditional Liverpool dish.
fact: A Scouser is an informal name for a person from Liverpool.
fact: «Boss» is Scouse for great, as in «Boss, your nan makes the best chips».
fact: «La» means mate or lad, and «ta» means thanks.
exercise: «Boss, your nan makes the best chips.» In this sentence, what does boss mean? | Angry | A person in charge at work | Tired | *Great
exercise: Scouse can mean a stew. What kind of dish is it? | A sweet pudding from London | *A meat and vegetable stew from Liverpool | A type of bread from Scotland | A fizzy drink
exercise: In Scouse, which word means mate or lad? | *la | ta | boss | sound
test: Jory says «ta» after you give him a biscuit. What does ta mean? | Please give me more | Goodbye | *Thanks | Sorry
test: Every accent is a proper way of speaking. What does this lesson teach? | Scouse is wrong, so use Standard English always | Only people in London have real accents | Accents do not matter at all | *Every accent is a proper way of speaking
exam: Read «Boss, your nan makes the best chips.» Which word shows the chips are very good? | makes | chips | nan | *boss
